meta {
  name: Report - TransactionList (created_by columns)
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{base_url}}/{{realm_id}}/reports/TransactionList?columns=tx_date,txn_type,create_by,last_mod_by,doc_num&start_date=2026-05-31&end_date=2026-12-31
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  columns: tx_date,txn_type,create_by,last_mod_by,doc_num
  start_date: 2026-05-31
  end_date: 2026-12-31
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

docs {
  Exact same report + columns syncCreatedBy() calls in
  internal/datasource/quickbooks/sync.go (syncCreatedBy, ~line 1797) to
  backfill qbo_created_by. Use this to check, at the source, whether QBO
  is actually returning a non-empty create_by for a given Bill/BillPayment
  — before assuming Foundry's parsing/matching is the bug.
  
  Response is a columnar report (Columns/Rows), not entity JSON. Each row's
  ColData has entries in Columns order; the txn_type column's `id` field is
  the entity's QBO Id (used to build the qbo-{type}-{id} external_id match).
  
  If create_by comes back empty/missing for a row here, that's a QBO-side
  gap (nothing Foundry can fix by re-syncing) — not a Foundry bug. If it
  comes back populated here but Foundry's qbo_created_by is still empty
  after a sync, that points at the prefix-mapping / UpdateTransactionCreatedBy
  matching logic instead.
  
  Narrow start_date/end_date to bracket the specific transaction you're
  checking — the report can be large over the full since_date window.
}
